**Cause:** generic classes are *invariant* in their type parameters by default. So `Ok[Dog]` is **not** a subtype of `Ok[Animal]` — mypy can't rule out that an `Ok[Animal]` holds a `Cat`. Therefore `Result[Dog, str]` (`= Ok[Dog] | Err[str]`) isn't compatible with `Result[Animal, str]`, which is what `decodeAnimal` declares as its return type.

**Fix:** declare the parameters covariant. With PEP 695 syntax you do this by prefixing the parameter with `+` — not with `TypeVar(..., covariant=True)`, which is probably what triggered your "covariant parameters" complaint:

```python
@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Ok[+T]:
    value: T

@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Err[+E]:
    value: E
```

Covariance is safe here precisely because the dataclasses are frozen: `value` is read-only, `T` is only ever produced, never consumed, so mypy accepts it (a mutable attribute would be rejected).

Now `Ok[Dog] <: Ok[Animal]`, unions are covariant, so `Result[Dog, str] <: Result[Animal, str]`, and `decodeAnimal` type-checks unchanged.
